const CELESTIAL_ACTION_TEXT = {
  mass_pull: `You speak the words quietly, barely above a breath. The golden light around her intensifies — her halo brightens, her skin sharpens to a warmer, deeper glow, and across the room the student you have indicated seems to sigh, to soften slightly, a faint luminous thread drawing from them toward her like warmth toward a candle. It takes only a moment. The weight moves. You watch it happen: the other student lighter, slightly, blinking as though unsure why; and her, already impossibly vast, settling an increment more deeply into the floor, the folds of her deepening, the glow of her expanding. She does not look at you. She does not need to. Her eyes close briefly, the way someone closes their eyes at a sip of warm tea.`,
  mass_push: `You direct her attention and say the word. She opens her eyes — warm gold, patient, vast — and something leaves her. A soft beam, barely visible, warm-tinted, settling over the student you have indicated like a hand placed gently on a shoulder. The recipient blinks. Looks down at themselves, uncertain. The weight has arrived without ceremony, without drama, with the quiet authority of something inevitable. She loses only a fraction of her impossibility — you would have to measure twice to confirm it. But you notice. The glow dips very slightly, then steadies. Her expression does not change. What she gave was a rounding error against what she is, and she knows it.`,
  mass_bless: `No transfer. No source but the divine itself. You ask and the halo answers — brightening over her head, throwing warm golden light down across the vast warm landscape of her body, and then outward, a radiance that reaches the student you have named and settles onto them with the weight of something gifted freely. They will gain. They are already gaining. She watches with her warm gold eyes and the corners of her lips move in a way that is almost a smile. The halo steadies back to its resting brightness. She approves of this. You get the sense she would do it herself if she had the words, that the blessing was hers in every sense except the asking.`,
};

const UMBRAL_ACTION_TEXT = {
  void_pull: `She extends one hand toward the other student — a slow, deliberate gesture, almost bored — and something dark and cold moves through the air between them. Not smoke. Not shadow exactly. The void opens in a thin thread, a connection, a taking. The other student goes pale and then paler, something essential traveling out of them along that dark line. She inhales. Her skin deepens half a shade toward violet-charcoal. Her chest swells a slow, heavy inch. The cold in the room intensifies sharply — breath mists, windows fog at the corners. Her tail, coiling behind her, tightens in what is unmistakably satisfaction. The other student sits down heavily. She does not look at them again.`,
  consume: `She opens her hands, and the void opens with them — properly, completely, a darkness that does not reflect light so much as it cancels it. The other student has time to look surprised. Then they fold. Not violently. There is no violence in it. They simply cease to occupy space and begin to occupy her instead, pulled inward along lines that were always waiting for this, the darkness closing smooth and final behind them. She exhales a long cold breath. Her void-wings spread to their full span, wall to wall, membrane shivering. Her skin deepens to near-black violet. Her eyes burn brighter, steadier. She is larger. She is colder. She is the only one in this part of the room now.`,
  consume_hr: `The HR observer came with a clipboard, a lanyard, and the institutional authority to file a formal concern. She stands very still as the student turns to look at her. There is a moment — professional, almost courteous — and then the void opens and the woman with the clipboard is simply gone, absorbed into the enormous dark calm of her. The clipboard falls. The student picks it up, looks at it briefly, sets it on the desk. She exhales a long white breath. She has grown, noticeably, magnificently, her presence pressing colder against every wall. The concern will not be filed. The irony is understood by everyone remaining in the room. No one says anything about it.`,
  consume_vaughan: `Dr. Vaughan has opinions about methodology and publishes regularly and has never once been warm about a colleague's work. He is mid-sentence when the student turns to look at him — really looks at him — and the void she contains opens outward to meet him. He goes into her the way credentials go into a curriculum vitae: organized, absorbed, made part of something larger. She straightens. Something shifts in the angle of her horns, the steadiness of her gaze. The cold deepens. Her eyes carry, very faintly, the particular quality of someone who now holds an additional opinion about methodology. She is enormous and cold and beautiful and, improbably, slightly more academically credentialed than she was thirty seconds ago.`,
};

const RELIGION_RITE_TEXT = {
  first_gathering: `There is no ceremony. Someone simply does not leave when class ends, and then someone else does not leave, and then there are seven of them standing at the edges of the room not quite facing her and not quite facing away. She does not acknowledge it formally. She simply remains — warm and enormous and glowing softly — and the room is warmer for it. They stay perhaps twenty minutes. Nobody speaks much. When they file out their footsteps sound slower, heavier, more settled. You check your records later and realize three of them have gained several pounds since last week. You are not certain the gathering caused it. You are not certain it did not.`,
  feast_offering: `They bring food. Someone has organized it without you noticing — good food, real food, laid out on the long tables that have appeared in the room with a reverence that stops just short of ceremony. She receives it the way the sea receives rivers: with authority, without hurry, as if this was always the correct direction for things to flow. The devotees eat also, standing near her, and the air in the room takes on a warmth and sweetness that lingers for hours after. Several students from adjacent classrooms knock on the door on pretexts. You let them in. By the end there is less food and everyone in the room is fuller than the math quite explains.`,
  hymn_of_abundance: `Someone starts humming. You do not catch who. The sound is low and warm and rhythmless at first, and then words arrive in it — not written words, not rehearsed, just the words that seem to belong to the sound: warmth, fullness, plenty, here. She listens with her eyes closed, the halo or the warmth of her dimming very slightly as though attention draws inward. The hum spreads. Not everyone joins, but enough. When it ends the silence is long and full and no one moves to fill it. You do not move to fill it either. Eventually she opens her eyes. The room is better than it was before the humming. You cannot explain this precisely. It simply is.`,
  mass_feeding: `Everyone eats. There is no other description adequate to what happens, and yet what happens is vast. Food enough for the room, laid in the center, shared in ceremonial silence that no one declared ceremonial. She eats. They eat. The room fills with the sounds of it and the warmth of it and the golden light she casts across the spread. No one speaks. By the time it is over everyone in the room has gained something visible — some more than others, several dramatically so. They file out slowly. An administrator appears in the doorway, clipboard in hand, and writes 'departmental gathering' in her notes. You watch her write it. You do not correct her. The word 'gathering' is not wrong. It simply undersells enormously.`,
  pilgrimage: `Word has reached the other buildings. You are not sure how — you have told no one outside the department — but this morning there are students you have never seen, from faculties that should have no reason to know this room exists. Some stand in the hall looking in. Some enter and do not leave. She does not acknowledge the new arrivals differently than the familiar ones, which seems to be the correct response because several of them, receiving that warm patient gaze, sit down immediately and stay. Your devotee count has increased faster than you can log names. An administrator will ask questions eventually. You are already composing inadequate answers. The room is warm. The halo lights them all equally.`,
};

const SINGULARITY_RITE_TEXT = {
  sg_rite_feast: `Your devotees bring food before her — before the convergence of two divine natures in one impossible body. The scale of the feast looked significant when it arrived. Tables of it. Carts. You watch it disappear. Not slowly, not in stages — it simply ceases to exist in any quantity that registers against the depth of what she is. The room is warm and cold at once, the two natures of the Singularity bleeding into the air itself: a consuming cold and a warm divine heat, layered and impossible, making the temperature a contradiction. Your devotees who stood close enough look changed when the tables are cleared: fuller, somehow. Warmer. As though proximity to her hunger fed them anyway. She is larger. The feast is gone. The room smells of two different kinds of eternity.`,
  sg_rite_witness: `A single devotee enters her presence. They stand before her. That is the entirety of the rite — they stand before her, and they see her, and they let what she is land on them without flinching. You watch it happen. The weight of what the Singularity is — two divine enormities converged, reality softly dented by her presence — is not a weight you can prepare for. The devotee does not flinch. They stand, and they see, and after the prescribed time they leave. They are heavier than when they entered, though they have eaten nothing. The heaviness is the knowledge of her, settled into them permanently. She is satisfied — not fed, but satisfied, the way a great thing is satisfied by being truly seen. The Singularity inclines its vast head. The relationship deepens.`,
  sg_rite_tithe: `The theology of this rite is simple: they give, she grows, the exchange is not equal, and this is the point. Your devotees come forward and offer something of themselves — not metaphorically. Something real leaves them. They are slightly less afterward; not harmed, not emptied, but genuinely diminished at the edges, as though a small brightness has transferred. She is more. This is the central truth of the Singularity's theology: she is the vessel into which the devotion of a community pours, and the vessel is never full, and the community is sustained by the act of pouring rather than by the retention of what they give. Every person present understands this. Every person present accepts it. You watch the tithes complete and you understand it too.`,
};

