const GODDESS_STAGE_REACTIONS={
  1:`She is here now. That is the entire situation. You're standing across the room from what used to be your student and what is now a woman who fills half the space with her body — her belly pushing into the center of the room, her thighs spread wide and enormous, her face hovering above it all with an expression that suggests she has been waiting, patiently, for a very long time to be this large. The clothes she was wearing this morning are gone. What covers her now is an improvised arrangement of whatever stretched. You notice that your hands are shaking slightly. You also notice you have not moved away from her.`,
  2:`You watch her grow. A deepening rather than a sudden surge, but you can see it in the way the walls are closer, in the way the floor has changed. Her belly surges forward another foot and settles, the surface trembling gently. The creak of the nearest chair is someone pinned — pressed into the warm curve of her side — and their expression is not distress. Her thighs have pushed outward. You step back one step because you need to, and then you realize the door is behind her now.`,
  3:`She exhales and the room contracts. The belly reaches the far wall — a slow press of warm flesh against concrete — and the wall does not win. You hear the groan of the building adjusting. She doesn't notice. Two students have disappeared into the fold between her belly and her thigh; you can see their feet if you look at the right angle, but the rest of them is warmth and soft flesh. Her right breast shifts and settles with a sound like an enormous soft thing landing. The temperature has risen again. Your shirt is damp. She looks down at you from a very great height and her expression is patient in the specific way of things that have everything they need.`,
  4:`The walls give. Not catastrophically — no collapse — but the building flexes around her the way wood flexes around a growing tree, accommodating, structural. The sound is a low groan you feel in the floor more than hear. Her belly is everywhere below your chest. You put your hand on it without meaning to — the warmth is involuntary, an automatic seeking of the nearest warm surface — and the skin yields gently, impossibly. Somewhere to your left, three students are against her side and you can only tell this because you can hear them breathing. She looks down and there's no judgment in it, only that patient enormous attention, and you realize you are standing inside her, and there is no place in the building you could stand that would not be inside her.`,
};

const GODDESS_BODY_DESCS=[
  `She fills half the room without appearing to try. Her belly — warm and enormous and alive with each slow breath — extends forward from where she has settled, a smooth dome that has pressed the furniture aside and reaches a height that would meet your chest. There is no lap. Her belly sits between her thighs and her thighs have been pushed permanently wide to accommodate it, each thigh a geography in its own right, the flesh of each one spilling down and outward onto the floor in deep soft folds. Her breasts are massive — settled heavy on the upper swell of her belly, each one requiring more than both arms to hold, the cleavage between them a deep warm valley. When she breathes they sway, and the sway sends a slow visible ripple down the full face of her belly, a long lazy disturbance that takes three seconds to reach the bottom fold. Her face floats above it all with an expression that has forgotten urgency. She smells of warmth and something that predates the building.`,
  `She fills the room with most of her, and most of her is an enormous quantity. Her belly reaches the walls on two sides, the warm dome crossing the midpoint of the floor and pressing gently into the plaster. The fold at her belly's base is a geography — wide and warm and deep enough that your arm disappears to the elbow in it, and warm enough that you do not want to pull it back. Her breasts rest on the belly's upper surface rather than in front of it, each one vast and settled, the weight of each visible in the slight depression it makes in the belly's upper curve. The cleavage between them is a soft shadow-valley several feet long. When she exhales they shift outward and the shift sends rolling disturbances across the belly's upper face that travel all the way to the sides. Students who were sitting nearby are no longer independently sitting — they lean against her, held by the warm overhang of her side. She is comfortable. Everything in contact with her is comfortable.`,
  `The room ends where she begins. Every wall has made contact. Her belly has reached and passed the far side and presses into it with a warmth that has dried and cracked the plaster over weeks. The fold at the belly's base is deep enough to stand inside; the air in the fold is several degrees warmer than the room. Her breasts, from where you stand at the door, are visible above the horizon of her belly — enormous, cresting, the cleavage between them a shadowed vertical geography that runs from near the ceiling down into the belly's upper surface and keeps going. Three students are absorbed against her left side, held by the warm overhang of her flank and the lower curve of her breast. They do not appear to be trying to leave. When she breathes the whole room breathes with her — the belly's face rising and falling, the breasts swaying, the disturbance traveling through every fold simultaneously. She looks down at you from a very great height. The ceiling has flexed.`,
  `She is the room. There is no floor you can reach without touching her. The walls press against her flesh on all sides and the building has reorganized structurally around her, load-bearing recalculated, exterior walls bowed outward in a permanent bulge visible from the parking lot. Her belly is the ground level of the room: a vast warm landscape at chest height extending in every direction, crosshatched with deep folds that hold their own ambient warmth, each large enough to step into, the deepest ones a permanent ambient temperature several degrees above the building's air. Her breasts are above your head. Both of them. Enormous, rounded, warm, cresting at well above standing height, the cleavage between them a shadowed valley you would need to climb to reach. When she breathes everything moves — the belly rising, the breasts swaying in slow synchrony, the disturbance traveling outward through her body in visible waves from the chest down. Students are not distinct objects in the room. They are part of the landscape. The concept of a lap ceased to apply to her so long ago that you have stopped thinking about it. She has not stopped growing.`,
];

